Steps to build a toolchain workflow with gem5, DRAMim3, CACTI and DRAMPower
carry out the simulation for dynamic and static power consumption by the DRAM banks. The goal is to compare the power for accessing data from a Cache as compared to DRAM bank for N bytes, for example, 64B, 256B, 1KB, 2KB, 4KB, 8KB. 

The microbench C code, contains the program that the C code executes [Explain the what the code does for this application] The C program isolates the memory behavior and it performs strictly sequential memory reads.

run_test.py
Path: gem5/
A python script defines the hardware in gem5. It creates a system, a simple CPU, L1 and L2 cache and connects DRAMSim3 as the main memory. 

cache.cfg
Path: gem5/ext/mcpat/cacti/
CACTI file describes the physical properties of SRAM and outputs the energy per read.

Scons build/RISCV/gem5.opt -j$(nproc)
To verify if build exists: 
ls -R build | grep gem5.opt
Build script 
./build/RISCV/gem5.opt run_test.py

A master bash script loops through data sizes of N. The hit counts and DRAM traces are extracted and runs through Cacti or DRAM power, and outputs the final CSV file that can be plotted to find 2KB break-even point.


